{"id":"gcse-atomic-structure","title":"GCSE Chemistry: atomic structure","subject":"Chemistry","label":"GCSE","exams":["gcse"],"language":"en","seconds":187,"duration":186.64,"audio":"voice.mp3","words":468,"sections":[{"id":"hook","title":"Inside an atom","start":0,"end":14.975},{"id":"explain","title":"The particles","start":14.975,"end":53.925},{"id":"explain2","title":"Two key numbers","start":53.925,"end":86.745},{"id":"example","title":"Sodium worked","start":86.745,"end":112.915},{"id":"check","title":"Your turn","start":112.915,"end":125.285},{"id":"answer","title":"The answer","start":125.285,"end":133.455},{"id":"mistakes","title":"Common slips","start":133.455,"end":152.375},{"id":"recap","title":"Recap","start":152.375,"end":186.64}],"lines":[{"s":0,"a":0.5,"b":4.96,"text":"Water, salt, the air in your lungs. All of it is made of atoms.","w":[0.58,1.38,2.02,2.28,2.46,2.54,2.72,3.6,3.78,3.88,4,4.12,4.32,4.5]},{"s":0,"a":5.26,"b":6.85,"text":"But what is actually inside one?","w":[5.28,5.46,5.64,5.8,6.18,6.68]},{"s":0,"a":7.3,"b":14.55,"text":"I'm Kora, and in the next three minutes you'll be able to describe any atom and work out its particles from two simple numbers.","w":[7.38,7.58,8.36,8.5,8.58,8.72,8.98,9.22,9.82,10.04,10.14,10.34,10.46,11.12,11.44,11.98,12.12,12.4,12.54,12.72,13.22,13.46,13.76,14.08]},{"s":1,"a":15.4,"b":21.55,"text":"Every atom has a tiny, dense nucleus at its centre. Inside sit protons and neutrons.","w":[15.48,15.86,16.12,16.34,16.46,17.06,17.48,18.04,18.14,18.3,19.38,19.82,20.08,20.72,20.86]},{"s":1,"a":21.85,"b":25.61,"text":"Electrons move around that nucleus in shells, like layers.","w":[21.9,22.58,22.82,23.14,23.34,23.86,24.06,24.9,25.1]},{"s":1,"a":25.91,"b":29.01,"text":"Together, we call these three the subatomic particles.","w":[25.92,26.62,26.8,27.08,27.32,27.66,27.8,28.4]},{"s":1,"a":29.31,"b":34.35,"text":"The nucleus is tiny compared with the whole atom, yet it holds almost all of the mass.","w":[29.34,29.48,30.02,30.18,30.64,31.04,31.18,31.3,31.58,32.36,32.58,32.72,33.16,33.68,33.84,33.94,34.06]},{"s":1,"a":34.65,"b":38.27,"text":"A proton has relative mass one and charge plus one.","w":[34.7,34.8,35.34,35.6,36.04,36.52,37.02,37.18,37.62,38.08]},{"s":1,"a":38.57,"b":41.61,"text":"A neutron has relative mass one and charge zero.","w":[38.62,38.74,39.28,39.52,40.04,40.44,40.7,40.84,41.22]},{"s":1,"a":41.91,"b":45.39,"text":"An electron has almost no mass and charge minus one.","w":[41.96,42.1,42.7,42.98,43.38,43.64,44.08,44.24,44.74,45.24]},{"s":1,"a":45.69,"b":49.69,"text":"Atoms are neutral because the positive protons cancel the negative electrons.","w":[45.8,46.1,46.26,46.74,47,47.12,47.6,48.14,48.56,48.7,49.06]},{"s":1,"a":49.99,"b":53.5,"text":"So the number of protons always equals the number of electrons.","w":[50.06,50.22,50.36,50.66,50.8,51.5,51.9,52.24,52.4,52.7,52.8]},{"s":2,"a":54.35,"b":58.25,"text":"Two numbers tell you everything. The atomic number is the number of protons.","w":[54.38,54.6,54.92,55.1,55.26,56.06,56.2,56.58,56.94,57.06,57.2,57.46,57.58]},{"s":2,"a":58.55,"b":62.27,"text":"It defines the element, so change it and you change the element.","w":[58.64,58.72,59.26,59.42,60.16,60.38,60.72,61.08,61.2,61.38,61.68,61.84]},{"s":2,"a":62.57,"b":68.38,"text":"The mass number is protons plus neutrons. So neutrons equal mass number minus atomic number.","w":[62.6,62.78,63.1,63.44,63.62,64.22,64.52,65.52,65.76,66.36,66.64,66.94,67.26,67.64,68.04]},{"s":2,"a":68.68,"b":74.49,"text":"Isotopes are atoms of the same element with the same number of protons but different numbers of neutrons.","w":[68.8,69.44,69.72,70.04,70.14,70.28,70.6,71.16,71.32,71.48,71.78,72.04,72.16,72.92,73.12,73.46,73.78,73.88]},{"s":2,"a":74.79,"b":82.08,"text":"Electron shells fill two, then eight, then eight, for the first twenty elements. Fill each shell before the next.","w":[74.84,75.38,75.78,76.06,76.78,77.12,77.9,78.18,78.5,78.62,78.76,79.26,79.68,80.48,80.78,81.02,81.36,81.62,81.74]},{"s":2,"a":82.38,"b":86.32,"text":"The outer electrons give the group. The number of shells gives the period.","w":[82.38,82.62,82.9,83.56,83.76,83.86,84.6,84.78,85.02,85.16,85.6,85.8,85.92]},{"s":3,"a":87.17,"b":91.54,"text":"Take sodium. Atomic number eleven, mass number twenty three.","w":[87.2,87.5,88.64,89.08,89.34,90.18,90.58,90.86,91.16]},{"s":3,"a":91.84,"b":96.44,"text":"Protons, eleven. Electrons, eleven, because atoms are neutral.","w":[91.88,92.9,93.84,94.76,95.24,95.62,95.9,96.04]},{"s":3,"a":96.74,"b":100.47,"text":"Check it: eleven positive and eleven negative charges balance.","w":[96.76,97.04,97.52,97.94,98.5,98.62,99.12,99.48,99.9]},{"s":3,"a":100.77,"b":104.17,"text":"Neutrons, twenty three minus eleven, so twelve.","w":[100.84,101.74,102.06,102.44,102.8,103.58,103.78]},{"s":3,"a":104.47,"b":107.68,"text":"Electron arrangement: two, eight, one.","w":[104.56,105.04,105.96,106.72,107.54]},{"s":3,"a":107.98,"b":112.49,"text":"One outer electron means group one. Three shells means period three.","w":[108.14,108.44,108.68,109.28,109.6,110.02,110.66,110.98,111.42,111.74,112.16]},{"s":4,"a":113.34,"b":114.28,"text":"Now you try.","w":[113.48,113.72,113.9]},{"s":4,"a":114.58,"b":119.75,"text":"Chlorine has atomic number seventeen and mass number thirty five. How many neutrons does it have?","w":[114.6,115.12,115.32,115.76,116.04,116.62,116.76,117.06,117.3,117.58,118.5,118.66,118.92,119.34,119.5,119.6]},{"s":4,"a":120.2,"b":122.56,"text":"Use the same subtraction we used for sodium.","w":[120.36,120.54,120.72,121,121.56,121.78,121.94,122.12]},{"s":4,"a":122.86,"b":124.86,"text":"Pause here and try it. I'll wait.","w":[122.88,123.22,123.48,123.6,123.9,124.38,124.54]},{"s":5,"a":125.71,"b":126.82,"text":"Eighteen neutrons.","w":[125.76,126.22]},{"s":5,"a":127.12,"b":131.53,"text":"Mass number thirty five minus atomic number seventeen leaves eighteen.","w":[127.22,127.54,127.8,128.12,128.54,128.88,129.28,129.84,130.4,131.06]},{"s":5,"a":131.83,"b":133.03,"text":"Well done if you got that.","w":[131.92,132.16,132.36,132.44,132.62,132.8]},{"s":6,"a":133.88,"b":135.2,"text":"Three things to watch.","w":[133.94,134.3,134.62,134.74]},{"s":6,"a":135.5,"b":141.15,"text":"First, don't swap the two numbers. The atomic number is never the larger one, and it counts protons only.","w":[135.56,136.12,136.36,136.68,136.78,136.94,137.76,137.92,138.4,138.72,138.84,139.06,139.2,139.64,139.86,139.98,140.08,140.38,140.86]},{"s":6,"a":141.45,"b":145.69,"text":"Second, isotopes never change the protons, only the neutrons.","w":[141.58,142.4,143,143.3,143.6,143.68,144.7,144.96,145.1]},{"s":6,"a":145.99,"b":151.95,"text":"Third, the first shell holds only two electrons, not eight. Fill it before you move outwards.","w":[146.06,146.76,146.92,147.3,147.66,148,148.24,148.44,149.3,149.54,150.3,150.52,150.62,150.96,151.1,151.4]},{"s":7,"a":152.8,"b":158.55,"text":"Recap. One: the nucleus holds protons and neutrons, with electrons in shells around it.","w":[152.86,153.78,154.34,154.48,154.98,155.3,155.84,155.96,156.76,156.94,157.52,157.66,157.98,158.32]},{"s":7,"a":158.85,"b":164.05,"text":"Two: atomic number counts protons, and mass number counts protons plus neutrons.","w":[158.92,159.7,160.16,160.46,160.78,161.7,161.88,162.22,162.48,162.76,163.24,163.52]},{"s":7,"a":164.35,"b":168.29,"text":"Three: isotopes have the same protons but different neutrons.","w":[164.38,165.34,165.88,166.06,166.2,166.44,167.1,167.3,167.66]},{"s":7,"a":168.59,"b":172.93,"text":"If you know those three things, you can answer almost any atomic structure question.","w":[168.66,168.78,168.94,169.16,169.44,169.68,170.36,170.48,170.7,171.04,171.5,171.7,172.1,172.5]},{"s":7,"a":173.23,"b":180.15,"text":"Exam tip: always write out the electron arrangement before you name the group or period. It is quick, and it earns marks.","w":[173.3,173.7,174.52,174.88,175.14,175.28,175.4,175.84,176.3,176.58,176.7,176.9,177,177.28,177.42,178.22,178.34,178.5,179.24,179.34,179.44,179.7]},{"s":7,"a":180.45,"b":186.04,"text":"I'm Kora, from Knowzone. Draw one atom tonight, and this will already feel easier.","w":[180.52,180.7,181.36,181.58,182.62,183.04,183.3,183.54,184.28,184.46,184.7,184.98,185.36,185.66]}],"scenes":[{"at":0.25,"heading":"Inside an atom","items":[{"at":0.4,"kind":"text","text":"Water, salt, air: all made of atoms"},{"at":5.16,"kind":"text","text":"What is inside one?"},{"at":7.2,"kind":"text","text":"Describe any atom from two simple numbers"}]},{"at":15.15,"heading":"The particles","items":[{"at":15.3,"kind":"term","text":"nucleus","detail":"tiny, dense centre; contains protons and neutrons"},{"at":21.75,"kind":"term","text":"electron shells","detail":"layers where electrons move around the nucleus"},{"at":25.81,"kind":"term","text":"subatomic particles","detail":"protons, neutrons and electrons"},{"at":29.21,"kind":"text","text":"Nucleus is tiny but holds almost all mass"}]},{"at":34.4,"heading":"Particle properties","items":[{"at":34.55,"kind":"term","text":"proton","detail":"relative mass 1; charge +1"},{"at":38.47,"kind":"term","text":"neutron","detail":"relative mass 1; charge 0"},{"at":41.81,"kind":"term","text":"electron","detail":"almost no mass; charge −1"},{"at":45.59,"kind":"text","text":"Atoms are neutral: + protons cancel − electrons"},{"at":49.89,"kind":"formula","text":"protons = electrons"}]},{"at":54.1,"heading":"Two key numbers","items":[{"at":54.25,"kind":"term","text":"atomic number","detail":"number of protons"},{"at":58.45,"kind":"text","text":"Change atomic number → change element"},{"at":62.47,"kind":"term","text":"mass number","detail":"protons + neutrons"},{"at":62.47,"kind":"formula","text":"neutrons = mass number − atomic number"}]},{"at":68.43,"heading":"Isotopes and shells","items":[{"at":68.58,"kind":"term","text":"isotopes","detail":"same protons, different neutrons"},{"at":74.69,"kind":"text","text":"Shells fill 2, then 8, then 8 (first 20 elements)"},{"at":82.28,"kind":"text","text":"Outer electrons → group; number of shells → period"}]},{"at":86.92,"heading":"Sodium: numbers","items":[{"at":87.07,"kind":"text","text":"Sodium: atomic number 11, mass number 23"},{"at":91.74,"kind":"text","text":"Protons 11; electrons 11 (atom neutral)"},{"at":96.64,"kind":"text","text":"11 positive and 11 negative charges balance"}]},{"at":100.52,"heading":"Sodium: particles","items":[{"at":100.67,"kind":"step","text":"23 − 11 = 12"},{"at":104.37,"kind":"step","text":"2, 8, 1"},{"at":104.37,"kind":"image","text":"Sodium: 11 electrons in shells 2, 8, 1","src":"sodium.webp","credit":"Pumbaa · CC BY-SA 2.0 uk · Wikimedia Commons"},{"at":107.88,"kind":"text","text":"1 outer electron → group 1; 3 shells → period 3"}]},{"at":112.92,"heading":"Your turn","items":[{"at":112.92,"kind":"question","text":"Chlorine has atomic number 17 and mass number 35. How many neutrons does it have?"}]},{"at":125.46,"heading":"The answer","items":[{"at":125.61,"kind":"text","text":"18 neutrons"},{"at":127.02,"kind":"formula","text":"35 − 17 = 18"},{"at":131.73,"kind":"text","text":"Well done if you got 18"}]},{"at":133.63,"heading":"Common slips","items":[{"at":133.78,"kind":"text","text":"Three things to watch"},{"at":135.4,"kind":"wrong","text":"Swap atomic and mass numbers"},{"at":141.35,"kind":"wrong","text":"Isotopes change proton number"},{"at":145.89,"kind":"wrong","text":"First shell holds eight electrons"}]},{"at":152.55,"heading":"Recap","items":[{"at":152.7,"kind":"text","text":"Nucleus: protons + neutrons; electrons in shells"},{"at":158.75,"kind":"text","text":"Atomic number = protons; mass number = protons + neutrons"},{"at":164.25,"kind":"text","text":"Isotopes: same protons, different neutrons"},{"at":168.49,"kind":"text","text":"These three answer most atomic structure questions"}]},{"at":172.98,"heading":"Exam tip","items":[{"at":173.13,"kind":"tip","text":"Write electron arrangement before naming group or period"},{"at":180.35,"kind":"text","text":"Draw one atom tonight"}]}],"check":{"at":125.11,"seconds":10,"question":"Chlorine has atomic number 17 and mass number 35. How many neutrons does it have?","answer":"18 neutrons (35 − 17 = 18)."},"fps":30,"mouth":"","level":"","credits":[{"title":"Electron shell 011 Sodium.svg","author":"Pumbaa (original work by Greg Robson)","licence":"CC BY-SA 2.0 uk","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Electron_shell_011_Sodium.svg"}],"voice":"qwen3-tts-12hz-1.7b (cloned voice)"}